WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.304 --> 00:00:01.470
(suspenseful music)

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.150
- What does the perfect plane look like?

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.890
Is it single-aisle, wide-bodied,

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.500
fuel-efficient, long range, or purple?

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.540
For each airline at this
year's Paris Air Show

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.610
the answer will be slightly different.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.710
That's why American
Aircraft maker, Boeing,

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.270
and its European rival, Airbus,

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.190
spends so much time and money

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.720
trying to ensure they're offering

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.880
a range of planes to airlines,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.380
and that's what makes this year's battle

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.410
for orders between these
two so interesting,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.570
because this year, Airbus is turning up

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.760
with a wider selection
of planes than Boeing.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.560
So, will that give Airbus the edge

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.810
in securing some of the hundreds of orders

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.610
expected at this year's show?

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:36.457
Let's find out.

00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:40.380
Okay, so first, a bit of context about

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.790
what makes this year's airshow

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.580
so important for airlines
and plane makers.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.780
See, while the COVID pandemic

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.390
may have decimated global travel in 2020,

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.460
it's since recovered in a big way.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.550
And now, with looming climate
targets and rising fuel costs,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.890
many airlines are looking
to renew their fleet

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.900
to buy more fuel-efficient planes

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.140
like Airbus' neo range
and Boeing's Max range.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.640
Some analysts say that there could be

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.700
over 1,000 orders at this year's show,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:06.690
but the decisions around

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.660
which plane to buy goes far, far deeper

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.510
than how many seats it
has or how far it can fly.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:13.830
- The analysis that goes into

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.300
a fleet decision for an airline

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.830
is one of the most critical pieces

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.600
of analysis that an airline does.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.250
- [George Downs] That's WSJ's Ben Katz,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.080
who I caught up with just before the show.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.089
- Their fuel consumption,
the size of the aircraft,

00:01:25.089 --> 00:01:28.230
the expense of operating kind of per seat,

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.210
these are kind of the
dynamics they're looking for.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.220
Also depends on, how quickly you wanna

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.120
turn around an aircraft.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.330
Low-cost airlines like
Southwest or Ryan Air in Europe

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.300
they wanna have utilization
rates of 12 hours a day

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.650
where their aircraft is flying.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:41.520
- [George Downs] For Boeing and Airbus

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.100
getting the right balance of
all these factors is crucial,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.570
because if they get it right,

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.160
they're not just selling a plane

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.950
but also, potentially
securing a lifelong customer.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.630
- Once you choose an
aircraft manufacturer,

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.070
especially, for smaller airlines,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.960
that's typically, the
one that you stick with,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.570
because in the cockpit you
know how you operate this,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.910
how you maintain aircraft differs

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.220
quite significantly
between Boeing and Airbus.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.290
- But above all, what
airlines seem to want

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.720
from an aircraft manufacturer is choice

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.970
and that's where Airbus might steal

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:10.470
an advantage at the airshow.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.090
See, Airbus is offering ranges

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.960
from its 100 to 150 seat A220

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.200
all the way up to the massive A350-1000,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.320
with a huge choice of seats
and ranges in between.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.540
And in the mix is the
company's widely popular

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.770
A320neo family of planes,
including the A321,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.420
which can fly more passengers

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.120
further than Boeing 737 MAX 10.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.800
And when it comes to Boeing's offerings.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.240
- Boeing's issue is that,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.150
while it used to have a
really big range of aircraft

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.690
those options have kind of
whittled down over the years

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.150
and part of the issue here is that

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.550
it hasn't been able to bring to market

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.440
some of the options that
it's been advertising.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.120
- In addition to dealing with the fallout

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.540
from the two MAX crashes in 2018 and 2019,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.820
the company has also
suffered years of delays

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.320
with its 777X planes,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.380
and production issues with
its 787 Dreamliner program.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.800
And this plane behind me, the 737 MAX 10,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.590
the one that could take on Airbus' A321

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:09.450
is still awaiting certification.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.740
All that has meant that Boeing

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.600
has a smaller offering this year.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.730
And we've seen some
airlines switching sides.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:17.610
- For example, the Iceland
Air has recently decided

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.430
to buy Airbus' A321

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:23.910
and that sets a precedent for the airline,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.070
which has been for a very long time,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.200
an all Boeing operator to potentially

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.360
switch its entire fleet to Airbus.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.220
- So, what does this mean for the show?

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.950
Is it the beginning of
the end of the duopoly?

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:37.410
Well, Boeing is by no
means out of the game.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.410
It's booked a series of
mega orders in recent months

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.660
including a deal to supply Saudi Arabia

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.310
with scores of aircraft

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.380
and it's not all plain sailing for Airbus

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.640
as the company has been battling

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.220
production delays on its A320 lineup

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.170
and has suffered a lack of availability,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.600
but nothing is set in stone.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.190
That's because the waiting time

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.530
for these new planes is over half a decade

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.870
as aircraft manufacturers
are still dealing

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.090
with supply chain woes, chip shortages

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.230
and production snails.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.260
So, even if an airline commits
to buying a plane today,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.810
there's nothing to say it
won't have changed its mind

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.862
by the time the next airshow rolls around.

00:04:11.862 --> 00:04:14.445
(upbeat music)

